Z||000000||BAM-BAM||01-06-2001||12:43 AM||||  Terry, I was wondering if you could tell me what all I need to do the 3800SC swap. I read through some of your old posts and was wondering if you could put together a list of what I will need to complete this dream. Right now now I'm balancing between the 3.4L HO and the 3800SC. Also what would the neccessary pieces cost, not including the power train and associated electronics? Is there a step by step that I could go by? It's time to get this ball rolling. I really, really, want the SC. The 3.4L is fairly basic and inexpensive (that's good). But I really, really, want the SC. If I can afford it, that's what I'll do. Please LMK.<P> Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT<BR>Eibach/KYB Susp.<BR>Mr.Mikes 500 series seats,headliner! MOMO Commando Steering Wheel, White Face Race Style Gauges And Soon To Be More!!! YEAH BABY-YEAH||||reg||12
Z||000001||terryk||01-06-2001||12:05 PM||||Oh, I thought you were challenging me to a duel to the death.....I choose rolled-up newspapers..<P>Well, you have to make a couple of decisions here.<P>1. What year 3800SC? 92-95 are Series I, 96 and up are Series II. Series II are more HP out of the box (205/225/240). Series I are easier to modify the chip. Series II is harder (at least I can't yet). A nice choice is a 94/95 Series I with a 92/93 pulley.  <P>2. What trans? If you're going to a manual, I can't really help. This is an important question. The 96 Series II with a 4T60E will run on a 94/95 ECM. If you go with a 4T65E (not any stronger), then you are stuck with a flash PCM (can't be readily changed).<P>I can only really help you with the information to install a 92-95 3800 with a 4T60E.<P>Let me know.<P>Terry<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 01-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||01-06-2001||12:52 PM||||NEWSPAPER! IS THAT THE BEST YOU CAN DO! COWARD! You could at least use the wet noodles!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 01-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||01-06-2001||05:45 PM||||Hey, I don't want to break a nail!<P>Ok folks, don't read too much into that....||||reg||12
Z||000004||BAM-BAM||01-06-2001||07:01 PM||||  Terry, I am looking into a SeriesII with a 4T60E. At least that is what I had planned. As far as the ECM goes, I thought that someone had all that figured out? I really want this mod for the added power and the the asthetic value. I will be doing most of the work myself, so that should save me some $$$. I was thinking that if I got the engine, trans, harness and what ever else I needed from the same donor car that it should be pretty much a plug and play deal (not counting the physical anomalies) with a little wiring changes to fit the Fiero instrumentation. Now of course I could be all wrong, however, this is the way I want to go. So, can you help a brother out? LMK<P> Thanks, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000005||terryk||01-06-2001||07:38 PM||||The only Series II and 4T60E combo stock is the 96. It is flash PCM, but it will run fine on the 94/95 ECM which is already worked out.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Overall, it's a pretty straight forward swap. You will need to buy the 3800 front engine bracket from Chris West, trans brackets and mounts from an A-car (Ciera, Century, 6000)with a 440, 4T60 or 4T60E, the driver's side axle from the same car, the passenger side axle from a 3800 U-van or A-car with a 4T60E, or purchase the shaft pn 26003925 from GM (about $35), swap on the manual trans outer CV's, slot the front cradle for a new mount position, the U-van engine side dogbone mount and fabricate the exhaust. The wiring is straight forward and all gauges work. You can go further (I added the cruise light and boost gauge from a Sunbird) if you want. <P>I recommend you call Redding Auto Wreckers and have them start watching for that combo or the pieces. Good prices and free delivery all over hell. <P>You don't *need* a 96 combo. You can get a 96+ Series II and a 91+ 4T60E from any of the A-cars, C/H, W, or U vans. I STRONGLY suggest you get an 4T60EHD from a supercharged car or the U-van. The benefit of the U-van is the final drive is 3.43:1. <P>Beyond that it's just little stuff to work out. The shifter is backwards (I built a mechanical reverser), add the PRND321 indicator (I used some from a Grand Prix - amber backlight and built a simple circuit to light an amber LED for each position), remove the 2/1 notches from the shifter and allow the extra throw to get first gear. Also, you get to cram a larger fuel pump in the tank. Easy.<P>I can handle any calibration changes for the 92-95 ECM's.<P>Feel free to email be at home.<P>Terry<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||terryk||01-06-2001||10:20 PM||||The only Series II and 4T60E combo stock is the 96. It is flash PCM, but it will run fine on the 94/95 ECM which is already worked out.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Overall, it's a pretty straight forward swap. You will need to buy the 3800 front engine bracket from Chris West, trans brackets and mounts from an A-car (Ciera, Century, 6000)with a 440, 4T60 or 4T60E, the driver's side axle from the same car, the passenger side axle from a 3800 U-van or A-car with a 4T60E, or purchase the shaft pn 26003925 from GM (about $35), swap on the manual trans outer CV's, slot the front cradle for a new mount position, the U-van engine side dogbone mount and fabricate the exhaust. The wiring is straight forward and all gauges work. You can go further (I added the cruise light and boost gauge from a Sunbird) if you want. <P>I recommend you call Redding Auto Wreckers and have them start watching for that combo or the pieces. Good prices and free delivery all over hell. <P>You don't *need* a 96 combo. You can get a 96+ Series II and a 91+ 4T60E from any of the A-cars, C/H, W, or U vans. I STRONGLY suggest you get an 4T60EHD from a supercharged car or the U-van. The benefit of the U-van is the final drive is 3.43:1. <P>Beyond that it's just little stuff to work out. The shifter is backwards (I built a mechanical reverser), add the PRND321 indicator (I used some from a Grand Prix - amber backlight and built a simple circuit to light an amber LED for each position), remove the 2/1 notches from the shifter and allow the extra throw to get first gear. Also, you get to cram a larger fuel pump in the tank. Easy.<P>I can handle any calibration changes for the 92-95 ECM's.<P>Feel free to email be at home.<P>Terry<BR>||||reg||1
